{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":7275344,"items":[{"institution":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04bdffz58","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"},{"id":"https:\/\/www.isni.org\/0000000121813113","id-type":"ISNI","asserted-by":"publisher"},{"id":"https:\/\/www.wikidata.org\/entity\/Q603034","id-type":"wikidata","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Drexel University","place":["Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, United States"],"department":["College of Arts and Sciences","Physics"]}],"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]],"date-time":"2026-07-30T08:37:48Z","timestamp":1785400668635,"version":"3.56.0"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Drexel University Libraries","license":[{"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/publicdomain\/mark\/1.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Static thermodynamic susceptibilities obey symmetries that have been known for a very long time. These are the Maxwell relations. Less well-known are the symmetry properties that are consequences of a certain type of perturbation. These are the Le Chatelier reciprocal relations. These involve relaxation of thermodynamic extensive and intensive parameters to new static equilibrium values after a sudden perturbation. Since time-dependent thermodynamic properties are governed by the fundamental equations of nonequilibrium thermodynamics, we expect the time constants of these relaxations to be governed, to some extent, by Onsager's kinetic coefficients. In this work we address the time scales and symmetries of these relaxation processes which have not been previously addressed. We explore the relation between the static susceptibilities (Maxwell relations) of equilibrium thermodynamics, the symmetries of the Le Chatelier reciprocal relations, as contained in the Le Chatelier matrix, the symmetries of the kinetic coefficients, which govern the relaxation dynamics of nonequilibrium processes, and the symmetries of the Fluctuation-Dissipation theorem.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.17918\/etd-483","type":"dissertation","created":{"date-parts":[[2021,7,16]],"date-time":"2021-07-16T01:17:20Z","timestamp":1626398240000},"approved":{"date-parts":[[2005,6]]},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Symmetries and relaxations in non-equilibrium thermodynamics"],"prefix":"10.17918","author":[{"suffix":"Jr.","given":"Anthony Joseph","family":"DeSimone","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04bdffz58","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"},{"id":"https:\/\/www.isni.org\/0000000121813113","id-type":"ISNI","asserted-by":"publisher"},{"id":"https:\/\/www.wikidata.org\/entity\/Q603034","id-type":"wikidata","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"DU"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Robert","family":"Gilmore","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Drexel University (1970-)"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"13980","contributor":[{"sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[null]}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,20]],"date-time":"2025-09-20T05:27:08Z","timestamp":1758346028000},"score":34.389595,"degree":["Doctor of Philosophy (Ph.D.)"],"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/researchdiscovery.drexel.edu\/esploro\/outputs\/doctoral\/991014632939304721"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[null]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.17918\/etd-483"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,11]],"date-time":"2026-03-11T08:05:42Z","timestamp":1773216342307,"version":"3.50.1"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>We investigate entanglement properties in non-compact SL(2, R) lattice gauge theory simulated via continuous-variable quantum circuits (CVQC). Employing the Iwasawa decomposition to separate compact SO(2) and non-compact radial degrees of freedom, we demonstrate that the weak-coupling regime induces radial mode freezing, yielding emergent compactification with a compact-like central charge c \u2248 0.87 and quasi-degenerate features in the entanglement spectrum (ES). This emergent regime parallels robust low-energy entanglement protected by higherform symmetries in compact Z 2 theories, where ES degeneracies sensitively depend on whether 1-form symmetries and Gauss laws are exact or emergent. Analytical theorems prove partition function factorization and twofold quasi-degeneracy in the ES under emergent compactification. Numerical simulations on small cylinders reveal structured ES with large gap ratios in weak coupling (\u2248 115), transitioning to reduced gaps (\u2248 1.56) in strong coupling, signaling radial activation and genuine non-compact behavior-analogous to symmetry breaking removing ES levels in topological phases. These findings unify higher-form symmetries across compact and non-compact paradigms, providing entanglement diagnostics for holographic RG flows. Implications extend to quantum simulations of (2+1)D gravity and gauge\/gravity duality.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6151126","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,10]],"date-time":"2026-03-10T10:15:15Z","timestamp":1773137715000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Emergent Compactification and Higher-Form Symmetries in Non-Compact Gauge Theories: Entanglement Spectra in SL(2, R) Continuous-Variable Quantum Circuits"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5716-6216","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Isamu","family":"Ohnishi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,10]],"date-time":"2026-03-10T10:15:15Z","timestamp":1773137715000},"score":34.07182,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"http