attice\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:alternatives>\n                        <jats:tex-math>\\mathbb{Z}_2<\/jats:tex-math>\n                        <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\" display=\"inline\">\n                          <mml:msub>\n                            <mml:mstyle mathvariant=\"double-struck\">\n                              <mml:mi>\u2124<\/mml:mi>\n                            <\/mml:mstyle>\n                            <mml:mn>2<\/mml:mn>\n                          <\/mml:msub>\n                        <\/mml:math>\n                      <\/jats:alternatives>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    gauge theory coupled to the Ising model, and compare them with their continuum field theory counterparts. One model has a (non-anomalous) non-invertible symmetry, and we identify two distinct non-invertible symmetry protected topological phases. The non-invertible algebra involves a lattice condensation operator, which creates a toric code ground state from a product state. Another model has a mixed anomaly between a 1-form symmetry and an ordinary symmetry. This anomaly enforces a nontrivial transition in the phase diagram, consistent with the \u201cHiggs=SPT\u201d proposal. Finally, we discuss how the symmetries and anomalies in these two models are related by gauging, which is a 2+1d version of the Kennedy-Tasaki transformation.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.21468\/scipostphys.18.1.008","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,9]],"date-time":"2025-01-09T06:22:27Z","timestamp":1736403747000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21468\/scipost.crossmarkpolicy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":43,"title":["Non-invertible and higher-form symmetries in 2+1d lattice gauge theories"],"prefix":"10.21468","volume":"18","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8491-1455","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yichul","family":"Choi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics"},{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/023dm3d04","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Simons Center for Geometry and Physics"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yaman","family":"Sanghavi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1294-2786","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shu-Heng","family":"Shao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0365-8225","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yunqin","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"C.N. Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"8907","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,9]]},"reference":[{"key":"ref1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1146\/annurev-conmatphys-040721-021029"},{"key":"ref2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.48550\/arXiv.2205.09545"},{"key":"ref3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.48550\/arXiv.2306.00912"},{"key":"ref4","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.physrep.2023.11.002"},{"key":"ref5","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.physrep.2024.01.007"},{"key":"ref6","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.physrep.2024.02.002"},{"key":"ref7","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.48550\/arXiv.2308.00747"},{"key":"ref8","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/B978-0-323-95703-8.00098-7"},{"key":"ref9","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/BF01049955"},{"key":"ref10","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/S0550-3213(97)00219-8"},{"key":"ref11","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.91.125119"},{"k